            <ArticleTitle>Ten year follow up of patients referred for coronary artery bypass grafting from a single district general hospital.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>584-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine the status of patients 10 years after referral for coronary artery bypass graft (CABG) surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Retrospective analysis of case notes from all patients referred between 1 April 1981 and 31 March 1985. Full information gathered from hospital notes, GP records, and Registrar General for Scotland.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">District General Hospital, West Lothian, Scotland.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">102 patients referred for CABG during study period. Cardiac surgery was undertaken in Brompton Hospital, London, Royal Infirmary, Edinburgh, and Western Infirmary, Glasgow.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">At 10 years after operation 32 patients had died (27 cardiac, five non-cardiac causes). Full data were not available for five patients. Of the 65 remaining patients 24 had no angina, 13 had had a repeat procedure (CABG or angioplasty), and 28 had angina.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Long term benefits of CABG surgery is disappointing. Further steps are required to reduce progression of disease in this population.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
